7. Automatic turn-around OFF

When the setting is disabled, the fighter must use the existing manual-facing rules.

This means:

* Running left can turn the fighter left.
* Running right can turn the fighter right.
* Directional attack inputs retain their original behavior.
* A Dash turns the fighter according to its existing rules.
* A stationary fighter keeps its previous facing.
* An enemy crossing behind does not automatically turn the fighter.
* Existing direction locks still apply.

This behavior must match the game’s original manual-facing implementation.

Do not leave behind partial automatic targeting when the setting is OFF.

The ON and OFF states must be genuinely different gameplay options.

⸻

8. Apply only to the human player

Automatic turn-around is a player preference.

It must apply to the human fighter in:

* Quick Battle — Regular Play
* Quick Battle — Custom Play
* Practice Ground

It must continue to work after:

* Fighter replacement in Practice Ground
* CPU replacement
* Removing the Practice CPU
* Restarting a match
* Respawning after falling into the Void
* Changing stages or characters
* Starting a new battle

CPU behavior

Do not apply this preference to CPU fighters.

Preserve:

* Quick Battle CPU intelligence
* Watch Mode CPU-versus-CPU behavior
* The CPU’s existing attack-facing system
* Practice Ground dummy behavior
* CPU movement and decision-making

The distinction must be based on the controller’s actual kind, not fighter identifiers, roster slots, or display labels.

A CPU must never inherit player-only automatic turn-around behavior simply because it uses the same Fighter class.

